What was built
You created a Terraform project that uses workspaces and a map variable to deploy EC2 instances with different instance types across three environments from a single codebase. The instance_type variable holds a map with three entries — dev gets t2.micro, stage gets t2.medium, and prod gets t2.xlarge. The lookup() function reads the current workspace name and automatically picks the correct instance type from the map. So the same terraform apply command creates a different sized EC2 depending on which workspace is active — no manual value changes needed between environments.
What was achieved
You created three workspaces — dev, stage, and prod — and deployed an EC2 instance in each one independently. Each workspace maintains its own separate state file so all three environments existed simultaneously without interfering with each other. The EC2 was placed inside a module keeping the code clean and reusable. After verifying everything worked correctly you destroyed each environment one by one by switching workspaces and running terraform destroy. The key achievement here is that one codebase with one ami value and one map variable was enough to manage three completely isolated environments with appropriately sized infrastructure — which is exactly the real world pattern used in production Terraform projects.